gtk: vmlab: the picture at the screen's own pixels on a scaled Wayland screen

GTK 3 knows whole-number scales only: on a screen the desktop scales by 1.25 it draws at 2
and the compositor shrinks that, so the guest's picture was resampled twice and soft. The
VMLAB_WINDOW window now shows the picture on a Wayland sub-surface of its own over the
drawing area (ui/gtk-vmlab-wl.c): the guest's frame as it is, in shared memory, with a
viewport (wp_viewporter) for its size in the desktop's units, at the scale the compositor
tells for the window (wp_fractional_scale_v1). GTK keeps the window, the title bar and all
input (the sub-surface has an empty input region).

- VMLAB_WINDOW_FIXED_SIZE=first offers the guest the drawing area's size in the screen's
  pixels (1536x832 units at 1.25: 1920x1040), and again, once, when the window fills a
  screen of another scale (maximized or full screen there, and settled: a window dragged
  across is small until it is maximized again, and until then the picture is scaled to fit).
  The picture starts on a whole screen pixel inside the window
  (mutter puts the window's surface on one, and samples a surface exactly only where it
  starts on one and has its own size), so a guest pixel is a screen pixel: measured on mutter
  46 at 1.0, 1.25 and 1.5, every pixel of the screen's picture equal to the guest's.
- VMLAB_WINDOW_SCALE_FILE names a file kept at the scale in percent, for whoever sets the
  guest's own scaling (win-venus.sh scale-watch).
- The host's pointer, which wears the guest's shape (0004), is as large as the guest means
  it: GDK shows pointer images at whole-number scales only, so at 1.25 the image is made 1.6
  times the guest's and shown at 2.
- VMLAB_WINDOW_NATIVE_SCALE=0 turns it off. Under X11 (GDK_BACKEND=x11) nothing changes.

The protocol headers are made at build time (wayland-scanner, wayland-protocols); without
them the file compiles to nothing and GTK draws the picture as before.

